 ],
 "digest": "This week we examine the devices and politics reshaping financial centres in the Global South. Coverage spans digital payment infrastructure—from Pix's regional integration effects in Brazil to India's forex derivative regulations—and the sovereign debt instruments constraining African financial autonomy, with cases from Kenya, Ethiopia, and Zambia illustrating both restructuring crises and alternative South–South credit flows. We also track institutional reconfigurations in Mumbai and Port Louis, where bank amalgamations and payment-hub positioning reflect shifting North–South financial architectures. A study of Ho Chi Minh City's cashless aspiration and analysis of Thailand's green macrofinancial regimes round out the week's focus on how financial devices, regulatory choices, and geopolitical alignments mediate subordination or agency in the Global South."
}